# Host-specific compose variables — copy to .env (gitignored) and edit.
#   cp .env.example .env

# Host TCP port for the web app and API. Leave 32400 unless Plex or another
# service still owns it; peer containers always reach plurxd on its unchanged
# internal port 32400.
PLURX_HTTP_PORT=32400

# Host UDP port published for GDM discovery. Plex clients only discover on
# 32414; change this only when something else (a still-running Plex Media
# Server) owns 32414 on this host. plurx then works fine by direct address
# (http://host:<PLURX_HTTP_PORT>) and discovery lights up once you flip this
# back.
PLURX_GDM_PORT=32414

# Physical machine's short hostname for Settings → Cluster. A native plurxd
# reads this from the OS; a container otherwise sees its generated container
# id, so set the same short name you get from `hostname -s` on the Docker host.
PLURX_NODE_HOSTNAME=${HOSTNAME:-}

# Authoritative database and compatibility root. A path on the host,
# deliberately: a named volume lives somewhere Docker chose and
# can be orphaned by a rename or a stack recreate, and plurxd handed an empty
# directory starts up showing the first-run setup screen rather than failing —
# which looks exactly like losing your library. Create it before first run,
# owned by the uid below:
#
#   sudo install -d -o $(id -u) -g $(id -g) /srv/plurx
PLURX_DATA=/srv/plurx
# Optional persistent-cache and disposable-scratch roots. Mount them in
# docker-compose.override.yml — the commented block in
# docker-compose.override.example.yml carries the matching volumes and the same
# container paths — and name the container-side paths here. An explicit scratch
# directory must be empty on first claim and must sit outside PLURX_DATA; plurx
# then holds an exact owner-only marker in it before any restart cleanup. It
# must be owned by PUID: a root owned by another uid refuses startup, while a
# PUID-owned root that is group/world-writable is repaired to 0700 with a
# warning. Setting PLURX_CACHE_DIR migrates no existing bytes — read the
# old-to-new path table in docs/OPERATIONS.md before copying anything.
# PLURX_CACHE_DIR=/var/cache/plurx
# PLURX_TRANSCODE_DIR=/var/tmp/plurx-transcode
# Keep the local replicated-read pool at 4 until retained 4/8/16 evidence
# proves a different value on the named workload:
# PLURX_CLUSTER_READ_POOL_SIZE=4

# Snapshot recovery and Docker startup readiness are one budget. The default
# 120-second transfer timeout plus three 45-second startup allowances fits the
# Compose/Dockerfile five-minute health grace. `make docker-up` resolves shell
# variables, this file, Compose overrides, and readable bind-mounted production
# TOML, then refuses an unsafe pair before it changes a container.
#
# You do not have to keep the pair in step yourself. Raise the transfer timeout
# — here, or in a production TOML this repository never sees — and leave
# PLURX_HEALTH_START_PERIOD unset: `make docker-up` derives the grace from the
# resolved timeout plus 135 seconds. Set it only to choose a grace of your own:
# any resolved value other than the tracked five-minute default is treated as
# chosen, honored as Compose resolved it, and refused by name if too short. For
# a named/opaque config mount, set the timeout explicitly or the checker safely
# assumes the source maximum. The supported maximum pair is:
# PLURX_CLUSTER_INSTALL_SNAPSHOT_TIMEOUT_SECS=3600
# PLURX_HEALTH_START_PERIOD=65m

# The uid/gid the container runs as. The default is convenient for a read-only
# media deployment. Permanent on-disk Dolby Vision conversion requires a
# dedicated host service account instead: set its numeric uid/gid here, make
# PLURX_DATA and only the opted-in library accessible to it, and do not share
# that identity with downloaders, organizers, or interactive shells. See the
# writable-media contract in deploy/README.md.
PUID=1000
PGID=1000
